From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bsp2020 <bsp2020@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 17:18:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 33/41] server: queue a checkpoint's state copy before the next
 batch, finish it after (LLAMA_CKPT_ASYNC=0: off)

A context checkpoint is taken right before the next batch is queued, and its target state copy (112.6 MiB of
recurrent rows on the production model) is device-to-host-link bound: ~7-9 ms with both GPUs idle, ~2 per request
and per agent turn, while the server then spends 10-80 ms building and queueing that batch.

llama_state_seq_get_data_deferred() queues the batched device copies and their per-group events as before and
leaves the host half (event waits + stage -> buffer copy) in the context; llama_state_finish() runs it.  The server
takes the target state of a checkpoint this way and finishes it right after llama_process() has queued the next
batch, so the copies overlap that host work.  They sit on the streams the next graph runs on, so the graph overwrites
the rows only after they are read.  A pending copy is also finished by the next batched state save or restore on the
context (state_stage), at the end of every update_slots() pass (any exit path), and dropped when the context is
freed; a failed read leaves nothing pending.

LLAMA_CKPT_ASYNC_VERIFY=1 also takes a synchronous copy just before each deferred one and compares the bytes once
it has landed: 85 checkpoints, all equal.  Outputs identical with the switch on and off (greedy, 4.2k and short
prompts, agent turns with checkpoint-restore branches); agent turns -11..-12 ms median (~1.3 %), short prompts
-3..-10 ms, 4.2k prompts -4..-6 ms.
